{"parentUuid":"136e74e0-b79c-4adb-a6f7-003cc5b602e7","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"abd7846675c5e14fb","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01HDfYqL7nutBUm73i2YBbep","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01ARDYjoyRKdKUa2cUxJBaBG","name":"WebFetch","input":{"url":"https://pysathq.github.io/docs/html/api/solvers.html","prompt":"Does PySAT expose a Learner / connect_learner / learn callback API for CaDiCaL? Can Python code receive every learned clause from CaDiCaL during solving? What about importing clauses via add_clause incrementally? Look for methods like add_clause, append_formula, conf_budget, propagate_budget that are relevant to incremental SAT."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":979,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1456,"cache_read_input_tokens":28824,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1456,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":52,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"}},"requestId":"req_011CaoouqqsA2DxPLh82WYHU","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"55eb01cb-e822-4e07-a3cb-1f6ae6b9474a","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:05:41.883Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"55eb01cb-e822-4e07-a3cb-1f6ae6b9474a","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"abd7846675c5e14fb","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01HDfYqL7nutBUm73i2YBbep","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01MfVUmqSCYmorM7ja76xxLK","name":"WebSearch","input":{"query":"\"learned clause\" sharing portfolio SAT solver persistence disk file format DRUP"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":979,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1456,"cache_read_input_tokens":28824,"output_tokens":504,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1456},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":979,"output_tokens":504,"cache_read_input_tokens":28824,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1456,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1456,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"req_011CaoouqqsA2DxPLh82WYHU","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"ecfc8165-8a13-4cdd-8cab-7e2f279e8253","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:05:42.523Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"136e74e0-b79c-4adb-a6f7-003cc5b602e7","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"56e72661-9c70-4718-ac89-40d9fd2179ff","agentId":"abd7846675c5e14fb","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013FsnTJjsFUg1utbvoJ2fES","type":"tool_result","content":"# IPASIR-UP Overview\n\nBased on the PDF content provided, I can offer the following insights:\n\n## What is IPASIR-UP?\n\nIPASIR-UP extends the standard IPASIR interface (a SAT solver API) to support external propagators. The document structures chapters around \"An Interface beyond IPASIR,\" suggesting it builds incrementally on the foundational IPASIR specification.\n\n## External Propagator Functionality\n\nThe interface enables several key capabilities:\n\n**Clause Management:** External propagators can both import clauses into the solver and export learned clauses back out. This bidirectional clause exchange allows external code to:\n- Inject domain-specific constraints\n- Retrieve inferences made by the SAT solver\n\n**Configuration and Monitoring:** The system supports \"Configuration and Management,\" with sections on \"Inspecting CDCL via Notifications\" and \"Influencing CDCL via Callbacks,\" indicating detailed control over solver behavior.\n\n## Performance Characteristics\n\nThe document mentions \"Experiments with SMS\" and \"Experiments with SMT,\" but the compressed PDF content doesn't clearly expose specific performance overhead metrics comparing IPASIR-UP to vanilla CaDiCaL. The empirical evaluation sections exist but aren't fully readable in this format.\n\n**Note:** This appears to be an academic paper on SAT solver integration; consulting the full publication would provide complete performance data.\n\n[Binary content (application/pdf, 797.2KB) also saved to C:\\Users\\22957\\.claude\\projects\\D--claude-pj-zmd\\7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9\\tool-results\\webfetch-1778184341511-ljo3yv.pdf]"}]},"uuid":"7861926c-9d1d-43a8-9cc2-e0d9ae973b31","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:05:47.405Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"136e74e0-b79c-4adb-a6f7-003cc5b602e7","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"55eb01cb-e822-4e07-a3cb-1f6ae6b9474a","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"56e72661-9c70-4718-ac89-40d9fd2179ff","agentId":"abd7846675c5e14fb","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ARDYjoyRKdKUa2cUxJBaBG","type":"tool_result","content":"# PySAT's Incremental Capabilities and Learner API\n\nBased on the documentation, **PySAT does not expose a learner callback API** for receiving learned clauses from CaDiCaL during solving.\n\n## What PySAT Does Support\n\nPySAT provides these incremental features:\n\n1. **Clause Addition**: The `add_clause()` method allows incrementally adding clauses to the solver's internal formula.\n\n2. **Formula Appending**: `append_formula()` enables adding multiple clauses at once, useful for incremental problem-solving.\n\n3. **Assumption-Based Solving**: The module supports \"assumption-based incremental interface\" where multiple SAT calls can reuse learned clauses from previous calls using different assumption literals.\n\n4. **Budget Controls**: Methods like `conf_budget()` and `prop_budget()` allow setting resource limits for incomplete solving.\n\n5. **Propagation**: The `propagate()` method performs unit propagation on assumption literals, returning assigned literals.\n\n## What's Missing\n\nThe documentation makes no mention of:\n- Learner callbacks or learned clause streaming\n- Methods to receive clauses learned by the solver\n- Any \"connect_learner\" or similar API\n\nThe design philosophy appears to treat \"solvers as _oracles_\" without exposing internal parameters like learned clauses, only providing the core SAT-solving interface."}]},"uuid":"392bf2aa-fb43-4b9d-a943-a5dcf18e9a60","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:05:47.770Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"55eb01cb-e822-4e07-a3cb-1f6ae6b9474a","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"ecfc8165-8a13-4cdd-8cab-7e2f279e8253","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"56e72661-9c70-4718-ac89-40d9fd2179ff","agentId":"abd7846675c5e14fb","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MfVUmqSCYmorM7ja76xxLK","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"learned clause\" sharing portfolio SAT solver persistence disk file format DRUP\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Validating Unsatisﬁability Results of Clause Sharing Parallel SAT Solvers\",\"url\":\"https://www.cs.utexas.edu/~marijn/publications/pos14.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - arminbiere/gimsatul\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/arminbiere/gimsatul\"},{\"title\":\"Scalable Proof Producing Multi-Threaded SAT Solving with ...\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2207.13577\"},{\"title\":\"SAT Competition 2016\",\"url\":\"https://baldur.iti.kit.edu/sat-competition-2016/index.php?cat=certificates\"},{\"title\":\"Revisiting DRUP-based Interpolants with CaDiCaL 2.0\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2501.02608\"},{\"title\":\"Conflict-Driven Clause Learning SAT Solvers\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/255409904_Conflict-Driven_Clause_Learning_SAT_Solvers\"},{\"title\":\"Revisiting DRUP-Based Interpolants with CaDiCaL 2.0 | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-90653-4_5\"},{\"title\":\"Streamlining Distributed SAT Solver Design - DROPS\",\"url\":\"https://drops.dagstuhl.de/storage/00lipics/lipics-vol341-sat2025/html/LIPIcs.SAT.2025.27/LIPIcs.SAT.2025.27.html\"},{\"title\":\"D-Painless: A Framework for Distributed Portfolio SAT Solving | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-90653-4_3\"},{\"title\":\"Unsatisfiability Proofs for Distributed Clause-Sharing SAT Solvers | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-30823-9_18\"}]\n\nI've found search results related to your query about learned clause sharing, portfolio SAT solvers, persistence, disk file formats, and DRUP proofs. Here are the key findings:\n\n## DRUP Format Overview\n\nDRUP is a proof format, and DRAT (Delete Resolution Asymmetric Tautologies) is the proof format that is backwards compatible with both RUP (Reverse Unit Propagation) and DRUP.\n\n## DRUP File Format Details\n\nDRUP proofs may contain lines with a 'd' prefix that refer to clauses (original or learned) that can be deleted without influencing the proof checking. In practice, the clauses that one wants to include in a DRUP proof are the clauses that are removed while reducing the (learned) clause database.\n\nFor disk storage, in the binary DRAT format, each clause consists of at least two bytes, with the first byte expressing whether the lemma is added (character 'a' or 61 in hexadecimal) or deleted (character 'd' or 64 in hexadecimal), the last byte of each lemma is the zero byte (00 in hexadecimal), and in between these two bytes, the literals of the lemma are shown as unsigned integers in the variable-byte encoding.\n\n## Clause Sharing in Portfolio Solvers\n\nA method has been proposed to produce DRUP proofs from parallel SAT solvers with clause sharing using a counting mechanism to track how many solver incarnations use a certain clause.\n\nGimsatul is a portfolio style parallel SAT-solver which physically shares clauses between different solving threads using separate watcher data structures for each solver thread and keeping the actual clause data immutable, allowing to share large clauses between threads through atomic reference counting.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"81910387-8b26-4040-a997-2266c2922dea","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:05:51.163Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"ecfc8165-8a13-4cdd-8cab-7e2f279e8253","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
